    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d357">

                <pb facs="0649" n="631" sameAs="frus:frus1951v06p1/pg631"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">411.56D31/4–251: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Indonesia</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cochran</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Djakarta</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-04-02T20:00:00+07:30">April 2, 1951—8 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1353. Rubber contract negotiations commenced this morning in conversations
                    between Sumitro,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d357-f1"/> Zain, Darmawan,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d357-f2"/> Osmaoen,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d357-f3"/> Wilson, Coerr
                    and MacFadyen.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d357-f4"/> After Indos had given usual
                    parade of difficulties inherent for them in meeting US desires, cordial
                    atmosphere was established and agreement reached that conversations wld be
                    pursued on basis (1) US wld offer commitment that US Govt wld extend good
                    offices to satisfy basic Indo civilian needs mfg goods (in accordance note
                    authorized Deptel 1042, Mar 27). (2) Quantity price and other details of
                    specific purchase contract wld be worked out mutually. (3) Indos wld also study
                    possible methods whereby Indo Govt cld implement govt to govt contract.</p>
                <p>Meeting this afternoon with Darmawan, Zain, Osmaoen and two Dutch technicians
                    reached no conclusions. Indos were given informal draft US note as mentioned
                    above and were asked to consider as starting point a contract to provide 200,000
                    tons in 18 months at 48 cents per pound. They spent whole session, however,
                    reiterating general arguments which they had advanced at London.<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d357-f5"/> Discussions to be resumed tomorrow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cochran</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d357-f1" n="1" xml:id="d357-f1-content">
                <p>Dr. Sumitro Djojohadikusumo, Minister of Trade and Industry and a member of the
                    PSI.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d357-f2" n="2" xml:id="d357-f2-content">
                <p>Dr. Darmawan, Director-General of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d357-f3" n="3" xml:id="d357-f3-content">
                <p>Member of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d357-f4" n="4" xml:id="d357-f4-content">
                <p>An employee of GSA and a member of the rubber purchasing mission.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d357-f5" n="5" xml:id="d357-f5-content">
                <p>The reference is to the London Rubber Conference.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
